        "title": "Soil Microbiology: Bacteria, Fungi, Mycorrhizae, Soil Food Web",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A gram of fertile agricultural soil contains 10⁸–10⁹ bacterial cells, 100–200 m of fungal hyphae, thousands of nematodes, and vast numbers of protozoa and arthropods. This community drives all nutrient cycling, builds soil structure, defends plants from pathogens, and regulates the atmosphere via CH₄ and N₂O. Managing soil biology is managing soil productivity."
    },
